    {
      "id": "chosen",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": -1180,
      "y": -60,
      "w": 940,
      "text": "CONTROL ROOM — the chosen direction\n\nDark, dense, keyboard-first. A tight table with one row per machine, status LEDs, monospace for every technical value, a persistent status bar, and the launch command docked under the editor.\n\nEvery value shown is a field the app already stores. There are no CPU or I/O graphs because nothing in the code measures them.\n\nTwo real limitations are surfaced rather than hidden: auto-detect only finds qemu-system-x86_64, and running machines are not restored after a restart."
    },
    {
      "id": "not-chosen",
      "page": "page-2",
      "x": -1180,
      "y": -60,
      "w": 940,
      "text": "NOT CHOSEN — kept for reference\n\nB · Workbench (top row): light, calm, master–detail, command preview always visible.\n\nC · Launcher (bottom row): bold card grid, one-click start, editor as a stepped sheet.\n\nWorth raiding for parts even though the direction went elsewhere: B's always-visible command preview and C's architecture picker with its native / emulated labelling both solve problems the Control Room screens solve less directly."
    }
  ],
